# Copy this file to `.env` at the repo root and fill in your own values.
# `.env` is gitignored; this example is not. Never put a real key in here.
#
#     cp .env.example .env     # then edit .env
#
# docker-compose.yml reads .env automatically, so no other step is needed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- generation
# Get a key from https://openrouter.ai/keys
#
# Only diagnosis-service reads this. It signs its own request to OpenRouter with
# it; no endpoint returns it and no frontend references it, so someone using a
# DEPLOYED site needs no key of their own - the server holds one for everyone.
#
# Deploying: supply it as an environment variable from the host (a .env placed
# on the server, a systemd unit, or the platform's secrets UI). Never bake it
# into the image and never commit it - this repository is public.
#
# Developing: use your own key rather than passing one around. The free tier
# rate-limits per account, so a shared key means you throttle each other, and a
# run that hits the limit halfway finishes with some hypotheses from the model
# and some from the deterministic fallback - an evaluation that looks complete
# and means nothing. `answered_by` is recorded per analysis, so check it.
#
# Without a key the system still works end to end: every /analyze answers from
# the deterministic ranking instead, and says so.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Optional. Defaults live in docker-compose.yml; override only if you mean to.
# LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter          # `ollama` runs a local phi4-mini instead
# LLM_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free
# LLM_FALLBACK_MODELS=qwen/qwen3.8-27b:free
